  "problem": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=(2-3x)^{3/2}$ about $x=0$ is",
  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{\u2032}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(2-3x)^{3/2} & \u27f9 &  & f(0) & =(2-0)^{3/2}={2}^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-3x)^{1/2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =-\\frac{9}{2}(2-0)^{1/2}=-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{27}{4}(2-3x)^{-1/2} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(0) & =27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(0),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & ={2}^{3/2}+(-9\\cdot {2}^{-1/2})x+(27\\cdot {2}^{-5/2})(\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3/2}(1-\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{27}{32}{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
